1
00:00:00,640 --> 00:00:09,860
Well, hey church family. Usually when we have a snow day Sunday, I don't have the time to pre-record a devotional or a message.

2
00:00:09,860 --> 00:00:14,120
We kind of usually call it later in the process.

3
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:24,680
And so I thought I would take this time that I have now to just record a little bit of a short devotional on the second week in our Sabbath series.

4
00:00:24,680 --> 00:00:35,760
And really just think it's such a gift for us to have this opportunity to kind of begin to apply this message series almost in a forced way.

5
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:42,200
Many of us are almost forced to stay at home and to have this period of kind of forced Sabbath.

6
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:53,880
And so I'm asking that you would see this as an opportunity and an invitation to begin to apply some of these things that we're talking about with the formational practice of Sabbath.

7
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:59,200
This 24-hour period where we stop and rest in the presence of God.

8
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:09,200
So I'm going to pray and then I'm just going to share a little bit of what I was studying and preparing for this week so that we don't kind of lose our place in the series moving forward.

9
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:09,640
Let's pray.

10
00:01:19,640 --> 00:01:36,680
Father in heaven, Father in heaven, I again just want to thank you for this opportunity to almost have a forced period of rest, of stopping the productivity and the efficiency and the busyness of life.

11
00:01:37,020 --> 00:01:48,380
And just having time to ourselves with our families and our communities to rest and to even just be confronted with the beauty of your creation outside of our windows.

12
00:01:48,380 --> 00:01:51,440
And so Father, we thank you for this time.

13
00:01:51,960 --> 00:02:07,620
And I just pray that whatever it is that you have prepared for me to say would come from you and whatever it is that each individual who interacts with this message receives, that that thing that they receive would ultimately be from you and not from me.

14
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:23,660
And so God, may we be people who are pursuing lives that are built on this rhythm of working with you and creativity and production and resting in your grace.

15
00:02:23,660 --> 00:02:29,960
So God, as a gift that you have given to us for your glory, for our good, and for the good of our neighbors.

16
00:02:29,960 --> 00:02:31,960
It's in Jesus' name I pray.

17
00:02:31,960 --> 00:02:33,060
Amen.

18
00:02:33,060 --> 00:02:43,420
So like I said, last week we launched this Sabbath series and we focused on the fact that Sabbath was a rhythm, is a rhythm that's woven into the fabric of creation.

19
00:02:43,420 --> 00:02:48,240
I've heard it said this way, that we are not human doings, we are human beings.

20
00:02:48,240 --> 00:03:13,040
And from the beginning God has modeled for us and invited us into this weekly rhythm of stopping from all our work, stopping from the constant activity, settling our thoughts about all the things that need to be done, all the problems that need to be solved, and just to be content, to have gratitude, to give thanks to the God who has given us this life as a gift.

21
00:03:13,040 --> 00:03:16,580
The very breath in our lungs is a gift.

22
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:21,400
It's where we cultivate this heart of contentment and gratitude.

23
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:35,740
And so today I want to move kind of past this idea of just stopping from working and dig a little bit deeper into this aspect of rest and how do we truly experience rest from God on the Sabbath.

24
00:03:35,740 --> 00:03:43,660
Because I think that there is a way in which we can stop working and yet not truly experience rest.

25
00:03:43,740 --> 00:03:54,580
There's a way in which we can put this spiritual practice in place in our lives so we can stop working for 24 hours and actually still not experience the rest that God intended for us.

26
00:03:54,580 --> 00:04:06,420
And one of my favorite pastors, not one of my favorite pastors, but a pastor friend of mine distinguishes these two activities by talking about the difference between relaxation and rest.

27
00:04:06,740 --> 00:04:13,020
And he defines relaxation as kind of turning ourselves off, turning ourselves off from activity.

28
00:04:13,020 --> 00:04:19,720
It's really what we talked about last week, just this idea of stopping, that there's a way that we can stop without truly resting.

29
00:04:19,720 --> 00:04:21,640
Relaxation is turning ourselves off.

30
00:04:21,640 --> 00:04:25,580
And he defines rest as plugging ourselves in.

31
00:04:25,580 --> 00:04:28,540
And the illustration he uses is that of a cell phone.

32
00:04:28,540 --> 00:04:31,800
Most of us kind of carry one with us everywhere we go.

33
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:35,340
Oftentimes it's a necessity for our everyday activities.

34
00:04:35,340 --> 00:04:39,440
And I don't know about you, but I've been in situations where I was out and about.

35
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:43,860
I needed my phone for a certain thing, but the battery was kind of getting down to a certain level.

36
00:04:43,860 --> 00:04:46,520
And I knew it wasn't going to last as long as I needed it.

37
00:04:46,520 --> 00:04:52,640
So I made the decision to turn the phone off to preserve the battery, to preserve the energy.

38
00:04:53,900 --> 00:05:01,380
And then I could turn it back on and it would be able to function for as long as it previously was able to function.

39
00:05:01,380 --> 00:05:10,380
And there's a way in which you can turn the phone off and retain the energy that's there without adding energy to it.

40
00:05:10,380 --> 00:05:14,820
And I think that that's kind of how Sabbath can function for us if we're not careful.

41
00:05:14,940 --> 00:05:20,220
It can be this thing that's more like turning ourselves off rather than plugging ourselves in.

42
00:05:20,220 --> 00:05:29,980
Oftentimes we get done with a period of what we would call rest and we feel just as depleted, just as tired, just as burnt out as we were before.

43
00:05:29,980 --> 00:05:36,780
If we aren't intentional with this practice, Sabbath can be a thing that provides us relaxation but not true rest.

44
00:05:36,780 --> 00:05:44,460
It provides us with a period of time where we're no longer depleting ourselves, but we're also not filling ourselves back up.

45
00:05:44,460 --> 00:05:50,020
And last week when we looked at Sabbath, we looked at it from the Ten Commandments in Exodus.

46
00:05:50,020 --> 00:05:58,960
And many of you probably know, some of you might not, but there's actually two accounts of the Ten Commandments in the first five books of the Old Testament.

47
00:05:58,960 --> 00:06:02,660
The first is Exodus and the second is in Deuteronomy.

48
00:06:02,660 --> 00:06:12,280
And if you look at these two accounts side by side, you'll see that the command for Sabbath is almost identical in both accounts except for two slight differences.

49
00:06:12,280 --> 00:06:20,220
And since I want all of us, myself included, to actually have time to practice Sabbath today, we're just going to cover the one difference.

50
00:06:20,220 --> 00:06:27,420
But at the beginning of the Exodus account, Moses tells the Israelites to remember the Sabbath.

51
00:06:28,040 --> 00:06:34,220
And we said last week that he was writing to this group of people, this group of slaves who were coming out of 400 years of slavery.

52
00:06:34,220 --> 00:06:37,100
They hadn't been able to keep the Sabbath.

53
00:06:37,100 --> 00:06:42,480
And so he's inviting them to remember this rhythm and creation that has existed from the beginning.

54
00:06:42,480 --> 00:06:48,360
Yes, you weren't able to participate in it, but now I want you to bring it back into the imagination of your lives.

55
00:06:48,360 --> 00:06:49,960
Bring it back into your memory.

56
00:06:49,960 --> 00:06:52,220
Build it into your life.

57
00:06:52,660 --> 00:06:59,700
And what he says in Deuteronomy, which is interesting because the context is to essentially, at this point, a different group of people.

58
00:06:59,700 --> 00:07:04,120
At the beginning, it's these slaves who have come out of slavery in Egypt.

59
00:07:04,120 --> 00:07:09,940
At the end, it's after 40 years of wandering where they've practiced the Sabbath every week.

60
00:07:09,940 --> 00:07:19,680
They've kind of been intentionally forced to practice the Sabbath every week in the wilderness with not a whole lot of opportunity to work or to co-create with God.

61
00:07:19,680 --> 00:07:21,560
They're just kind of wandering in the desert.

62
00:07:21,560 --> 00:07:30,360
And as they're about to come out of the desert and go into the promised land filled with endless potential, endless opportunity, endless work to be done,

63
00:07:30,360 --> 00:07:38,660
filled with a group of non-Jewish people who are not going to be keeping the Sabbath, the adjective changes.

64
00:07:38,660 --> 00:07:40,660
He doesn't tell them to remember the Sabbath.

65
00:07:40,660 --> 00:07:43,560
He tells them to guard the Sabbath.

66
00:07:43,560 --> 00:07:46,660
He says, guard this day.

67
00:07:46,660 --> 00:07:48,800
Keep watch over it.

68
00:07:48,800 --> 00:07:51,300
Essentially, he says, there will be a million distractions.

69
00:07:51,300 --> 00:07:54,180
There will be a million things to be done.

70
00:07:54,180 --> 00:07:57,460
There will be a million reasons for you not to Sabbath.

71
00:07:57,460 --> 00:08:02,840
And even when you are Sabbathing, there will be a million things going on in your head that you will be thinking,

72
00:08:02,840 --> 00:08:05,060
what if I could have been doing this instead?

73
00:08:05,060 --> 00:08:07,280
What am I missing out on?

74
00:08:07,280 --> 00:08:11,620
What am I losing traction on by taking this day off?

75
00:08:12,260 --> 00:08:24,740
And I think that if we have a creative imagination, this context, this situation that they are entering as they enter the promised land looks a whole lot like our circumstances today.

76
00:08:25,300 --> 00:08:40,960
Many of us are filled with endless potential, opportunity, work to be done, and we're surrounded by a culture of people who do not see this 24-hour period of rest per week as a value to be built their lives on.

77
00:08:41,020 --> 00:08:43,460
We're surrounded by people who don't value rest.

78
00:08:43,460 --> 00:08:47,500
And we're filled with endless opportunities to chase after.

79
00:08:47,500 --> 00:08:50,620
And so I think we need the same encouragement.

80
00:08:50,620 --> 00:08:52,580
We need to guard the Sabbath.

81
00:08:52,580 --> 00:08:57,820
We need to keep a close eye on what we are doing or not doing on the Sabbath.

82
00:08:57,820 --> 00:09:06,940
Not just the 24-hour period, hour of time, but what is it that we are doing to experience rest during this 24-hour period.

83
00:09:06,940 --> 00:09:19,700
And my hunch is that for most of us that have opportunities and potential and temptation to be distracted, even if we succeed at stopping for 24 hours,

84
00:09:19,700 --> 00:09:27,540
that on those 24 hours are going to be constant distractions and interruptions that are going to be pulling us away from experiencing rest.

85
00:09:28,560 --> 00:09:36,640
And I think even if we choose to keep the Sabbath, that our temptation might be to rush into the Sabbath running on fumes.

86
00:09:36,640 --> 00:09:45,580
And if we aren't careful, this posture will often lead us to crave the experience of relaxation rather than the experience of true rest.

87
00:09:45,580 --> 00:09:49,120
We're going to want to just unplug from the stressful realities of life.

88
00:09:49,120 --> 00:09:55,220
Maybe have a few cocktails, or if that's not your thing, a few too many little Debbie Cakes.

89
00:09:55,920 --> 00:10:00,160
Binge watch our favorite show while mindlessly scrolling on YouTube or social media.

90
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:06,440
Or pitter-patter around the house and fix all of the things that we haven't had time to fix.

91
00:10:06,440 --> 00:10:11,820
This can often feel like rest, but I think it's actually a form of relaxation.

92
00:10:13,560 --> 00:10:21,460
And oftentimes if we approach the Sabbath this way, when we head back into our normal life the next day, we don't feel rested.

93
00:10:21,460 --> 00:10:28,820
We don't feel ready or overflowing to chase after the creativity that God's inviting us into.

94
00:10:28,820 --> 00:10:30,380
We're actually still running on fumes.

95
00:10:30,380 --> 00:10:33,160
We're like the phone who had been turned off for a period of time.

96
00:10:33,160 --> 00:10:38,160
We turned back on with just the amount of energy that we came into Sabbath with.

97
00:10:38,160 --> 00:10:45,340
I think relaxation is what happens when we don't guard the Sabbath, when we don't keep watch over it.

98
00:10:45,340 --> 00:10:49,280
Relaxation is the result of this, I earn this mentality.

99
00:10:49,280 --> 00:10:54,000
We work hard for six days and we get to this period of rest and we say, I earned this.

100
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:58,160
This is my reward for the frantic pace I've been living.

101
00:10:59,100 --> 00:11:06,180
And what I would say on the other side of the coin is that rest isn't something that we earn, but it is a gift that has been given to us.

102
00:11:06,180 --> 00:11:09,420
It isn't a reward we give ourselves at the end of a long week.

103
00:11:09,420 --> 00:11:17,040
It is a gift that God gives us and that we're intended to work from, not work for.

104
00:11:17,040 --> 00:11:18,040
I would say it this way.

105
00:11:18,040 --> 00:11:22,180
If we don't work from rest, we will work for relaxation.

106
00:11:23,920 --> 00:11:31,440
If rest isn't this gift that we're receiving, that God is filling us up with His life and His presence and His goodness,

107
00:11:31,440 --> 00:11:38,300
and then we are able to turn around the next day and kind of have a week's full of supply of energy to pour out,

108
00:11:38,300 --> 00:11:45,560
then what will happen is we will actually just work six days on fumes, run into a spot where we get some relaxation.

109
00:11:47,460 --> 00:11:54,300
So my encouragement for us this morning is that we would just continue to lean into this practice of Sabbath.

110
00:11:54,300 --> 00:12:01,460
And as we do, it wouldn't just be something that we remember to stop for 24 hours or we remember to stop for a period of time to take a break,

111
00:12:01,460 --> 00:12:05,640
to not overwork, to not overproduce, to not be overly consumed with efficiency,

112
00:12:05,640 --> 00:12:11,880
but that we would also guard that time, that we would think about what it is that we are doing in that time.

113
00:12:11,880 --> 00:12:14,380
Keep a close eye on what we're doing on the Sabbath.

114
00:12:14,380 --> 00:12:20,740
Is it producing in us rest that will fuel our kingdom work for the rest of the week?

115
00:12:20,740 --> 00:12:27,060
Or is it just the turning ourselves off from the realities of life for a period of time?

116
00:12:27,060 --> 00:12:32,360
Now, I'm not saying you can't watch TV or have a glass of wine or eat some ice cream on Sabbath.

117
00:12:32,360 --> 00:12:33,420
That's not what I'm saying.

118
00:12:33,740 --> 00:12:39,740
I'm just saying I think the invitation for us is to guard our time of rest.

119
00:12:39,740 --> 00:12:52,000
To really keep watch over what we're doing during the practice to examine whether or not it's leading us to an experience of rest as a gift or relaxation as a reward.

120
00:12:53,580 --> 00:13:00,040
So that's my kind of encouragement for us today is we have this forced time of rest.

121
00:13:00,040 --> 00:13:05,160
Keep an eye on what it is that you feel drawn to do during this time of rest.

122
00:13:05,160 --> 00:13:12,160
If there's too much silence, are you drawn to be distracted to go do a chore or something like that?

123
00:13:12,800 --> 00:13:14,740
What is it that you're drawn to?

124
00:13:14,740 --> 00:13:23,740
Keep an eye on it and ask yourself, is this going to fill me up or is this just going to distract me to keep me from actually experiencing rest?

125
00:13:23,740 --> 00:13:26,980
This is my encouragement for us this morning.

126
00:13:26,980 --> 00:13:32,780
The curriculum that we're using, Practicing the Way, they have a podcast that's kind of a bonus podcast.

127
00:13:34,180 --> 00:13:42,120
And the episode on this week gets really specific about kind of internal and external challenges that will keep us from experiencing rest.

128
00:13:42,120 --> 00:13:46,040
And so I'm going to link that here in this video.

129
00:13:46,040 --> 00:13:55,820
And I would encourage you, if you want some practical, concrete ideas about how to truly prioritize rest during your Sabbath, how to guard it, how to keep watch over it.

130
00:13:55,820 --> 00:13:57,900
I'd encourage you to check that out sometime this week.

131
00:13:59,820 --> 00:14:05,020
But other than that, I'm thankful that you've taken the time to spend with us today.

132
00:14:05,020 --> 00:14:21,580
And I hope that you will redeem this opportunity to rest and to, again, just to begin to apply some of these Sabbath principles on this snow day to really be present to yourself, to God, to your family.

133
00:14:21,580 --> 00:14:22,560
Let me pray for you.

134
00:14:22,560 --> 00:14:29,660
Father, thank you for each person that has consumed this content with us, for each person that finds,

135
00:14:29,820 --> 00:14:32,460
a space in our community as a church.

136
00:14:32,460 --> 00:14:42,660
I pray that we would be a people that guard our time of rest as a rhythm of creation that you have invited us to participate with you in.

137
00:14:42,660 --> 00:14:51,240
God, help us to see where we're too obsessed with productivity, efficiency, with making money, with getting to the next thing.

138
00:14:52,100 --> 00:15:00,000
And God, help us to receive your invitation, not to work to earn rest, but to receive rest as a gift.

139
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:13,840
So, God, may we be a people who grow in our ability to receive rest as a gift from you, for your glory, for our good, for the good of our neighbor.

140
00:15:13,840 --> 00:15:15,200
It's in Jesus' name I pray.

141
00:15:15,200 --> 00:15:15,920
Amen.

142
00:15:15,920 --> 00:15:16,880
All right, church.

143
00:15:16,880 --> 00:15:18,780
Hopefully I will see you.
